# integrity.json pins SHA-256 over on-disk bytes, so every checkout must be
# byte-identical on every platform — LF everywhere, no exceptions. The Windows
# .ps1 executors self-verify their own byte content (hash-of-self, exact-length
# here-strings), so CRLF would break their native self-test; they run under
# PowerShell 7, which reads LF fine. This is the deliberate reason roundhouse
# does NOT follow the usual "CRLF for Windows script types" convention.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets: never touch their bytes.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
